erceived average—is a form of gender bias that’s generally unacceptable in modern education. So I’m not saying that average developmental differences are a “wild claim”; I’m saying that even if they exist, they don’t justify a blanket, gendered policy for assigning roles. A better approach is to assess each student individually, provide scaffolding (clear instructions, check-ins, co-responsibility), and give everyone a fair shot to practice being responsible—because that’s how they actually grow.
